<form class="totalform help-on-hover help-box" data-method="POST" data-route="/admin/settings/oauth"><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: enabled;"><label for="field-UID">Enable OAuth Server</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="enabled" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Enable OAuth Server</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Master switch for the OAuth 2.1 authorization server. When off, the OAuth discovery documents and endpoints return 404, so MCP clients that see a discoverable OAuth server (Claude, Claude Desktop) connect anonymously to the public tier instead of demanding a login. Turn off to run a public-only MCP site — access is then anonymous (public collections) or API key (admin). Existing access tokens expire naturally (default 1 hour) and cannot be refreshed.</p></div><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: dynamicRegistration;"><label for="field-UID">Allow Dynamic Registration</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="dynamicRegistration" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID"/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Allow Dynamic Registration</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Enable RFC 7591 self-registration at <code>POST /oauth/register</code>. Off by default — it's an unauthenticated endpoint that writes server state. MCP clients (Claude Desktop, Cursor) need this on to connect without manual setup. Left off, create admin static clients for each integration. Self-registration alone never grants data access (a registered client still needs admin consent), but an open endpoint invites client-record flooding and consent-phishing.</p></div><div class="form-field text-field " data-type="text" style="--grid-area: accessTokenTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Access Token Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><input type="text" id="field-UID" name="accessTokenTtl" placeholder="PT1H" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="PT1H" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long an access token is valid. PHP <code>DateInterval</code> spec — e.g. <code>PT1H</code> (1 hour), <code>PT15M</code> (15 minutes), <code>P1D</code> (1 day). Shorter = better security (revocation more effective) but more refresh round-trips. Default 1h is the OAuth common middle ground.</p></div><div class="form-field text-field " data-type="text" style="--grid-area: refreshTokenTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Refresh Token Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><input type="text" id="field-UID" name="refreshTokenTtl" placeholder="P30D" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="P30D" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long a refresh token can be used to obtain new access tokens. After this, the user must re-authorize via the consent screen. Common values: <code>P30D</code> (30 days), <code>P90D</code> (90 days), <code>P1Y</code> (1 year). Default 30 days.</p></div><div class="form-field text-field " data-type="text" style="--grid-area: authCodeTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Authorization Code Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><input type="text" id="field-UID" name="authCodeTtl" placeholder="PT10M" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="PT10M" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long an authorization code is valid before it must be exchanged for tokens. Short by design — the user is mid-flow. RFC 6749 §4.1.2 recommends 10 minutes maximum. <code>PT10M</code> default.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field " data-type="number" style="--grid-area: tokenEndpointLimit;"><label for="field-UID">Token Endpoint Rate Limit (per minute)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="tokenEndpointLimit" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="60" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="60" min="0" max="6000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Maximum requests to <code>/oauth/token</code> per minute from a single IP. Protects against brute-force code exchange and runaway refresh loops. Applies to all callers (admin requests are NOT exempt). Set to <code>0</code> to disable. Default 60.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: dynamicRegistrationLimit; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;dynamicRegistration&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Dynamic Registration Rate Limit (per hour)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="dynamicRegistrationLimit" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="10" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="10" min="0" max="1000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Maximum requests to <code>/oauth/register</code> per hour from a single IP. Dynamic registration is the abuse vector for client-record flooding — an unauthenticated endpoint that creates persistent server state. Default 10/hour by design. Set to <code>0</code> to disable.</p></div><div class="form-inline-fields"><button type="submit" class="button btn cms-save cms-button">Save Settings</button></div></form>